Z||000000||kevin||02-12-2001||02:20 AM||||I need to know which one of you guys can and or recommend a place where I can have my 85 Duke balanced/blueprinted to a respectable 'streeter'. I want to make approximately 130 h.p. (+) or (-). What would a decent price be? How long would such an undertaking take?||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||02-12-2001||09:04 AM||||I suggest the phone book. I don't know much about hopping up a duke, all my work has been w/ the v6 and old v8s, but I imagine boring it out, a good vavle job, 0 the deck, port the exhaust. Most of that stuff you can get done at any good mechinest.||||reg||1
Z||000002||151 in. of love||02-12-2001||06:05 PM||||I bored out 60 over<BR>Disassembled head<BR>refaced seats<BR>resurfaced valves<BR>shaved 6 off the head<BR>and reassembled with new pistons <BR>and cam.<BR>That only took me about 8 weeks.<BR>Not working weekends.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Motorhead||02-12-2001||07:22 PM||||A good sight to aid in your research is at:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.homestead.com/fiero2m4/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.homestead.com/fiero2m4/index.html</A> <P>Great advice on "hopping up" the Duke.<P>Pay particular attention to that red area on your tach at 5000 RPM.  Thats the "broken Parts" area.  They mean it!!<BR>From what I've gathered from old articles from Hot Rod Magazine August 1986, 110 HP is attainable with few hi po parts.  140 HP if you're ready to spend some cash.  Or if you have really deep pockets, you can run 9.70 et's at 135 MPH with a 376 HP Super Duty Duke.  The more you spend, the faster you go.<BR>Mike<P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10234998&p=38686771&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
